abstract: >-
  We study the positive solutions of Weil's explicit formula with the archimedean data of the Riemann zeta
  function: pairs of positive measures, one on the zero side and one on the prime side beyond log 2/(2π). By
  linear-programming duality such pairs exist if and only if a positivity statement involving neither zeros nor
  primes holds; its slack κ* gives the optimal degree-one explicit-formula conductor bound q_min = exp(−2πκ*). We
  conjecture that ζ's own pair is the only solution; this does not imply the Riemann hypothesis, but makes it
  equivalent to existence. We prove it when the zero measure lives on the zeros of ζ and the origin, whatever the
  prime measure, and when it lives on the zeros plus countably many summable points with the prime measure on the
  Bondarenko–Radchenko–Seip nodes. Certificates of the form Ξ²(H + ε·exp(−πt²)), with H ≥ 1 a polynomial in t²,
  vanish at every zero of ζ and show unconditionally that κ* ≤ 1.1508391·10⁻⁹⁰⁶ and that every solution has
  ∫Ξ² dμ ≤ 3.21·10⁻⁹⁰⁶. Uniqueness would follow from asymptotic properties of such functions defined by Hermite
  interpolation at the prime powers.
